.TH TALLYSTAT 1 "September 2026" "tallystat 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tallystat \- terminal-based graphical system monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tallystat
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tallystat
shows live CPU and memory graphs, network activity, disk usage and I/O,
hardware temperatures and a process list in the terminal. It reads
.I /proc
and
.I /sys
directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-color =\fINAME\fR
Color scheme: default, ember, monokai, solarized or nord.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-minimal
Only show the CPU, memory and process widgets.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rate =\fISECONDS\fR
Refresh interval (0.1 to 3600, default 1).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-averagecpu
Show the average CPU usage line.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-percpu
Show one line per CPU core. With neither \-a nor \-p, per-core lines are
shown on machines with up to 8 cores and the average otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-statusbar
Show hostname, time and uptime on the bottom line.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fahrenheit
Show temperatures in Fahrenheit.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-graphscale =\fIN\fR
Horizontal graph scale from 1 to 40 (default 7).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface =\fINAME\fR
Only measure the given network interface. By default every interface
except loopback is summed.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list\-colors
List the color schemes and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B q\fR, \fBCtrl-c
Quit.
.TP
.B k\fR, \fBUp\fR / \fBj\fR, \fBDown
Move the process cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl-u\fR / \fBCtrl-d
Half page up / down.
.TP
.B Ctrl-b\fR, \fBPgUp\fR / \fBCtrl-f\fR, \fBPgDn
Full page up / down.
.TP
.B gg\fR, \fBHome\fR / \fBG\fR, \fBEnd
Jump to the top / bottom.
.TP
.B Tab
Toggle grouping processes by name.
.TP
.B dd
Send SIGTERM to the selected process, or to every process in the selected group.
.TP
.B /
Filter processes by name or PID. Enter keeps the filter, Esc clears it.
.TP
.B c\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBp
Sort by CPU, memory or PID.
.TP
.B h\fR / \fBl
Scale graphs in / out.
.TP
.B ?
Toggle the help window.
.PP
Clicking a process selects it; the mouse wheel scrolls the list.
.SH NOTES
Process CPU% is relative to one core, so a multi-threaded process can
exceed 100%. Memory usage counts memory in use as MemTotal minus
MemAvailable.
